{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfiFs5VfiDRe7VY52yHFm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote all three files for candidate 1, and the self-check passes: it printed `OK`, with a finite ELPD-LOO of −2807.3 on its short smoke fit.\n\n**Model name:** `person_signed_switch_preference`\n\n**Hypothesis:** People judge randomness by one cue, how often a sequence switches between heads and tails. They disagree about which direction looks random. Most people take frequent switching as the mark of a fair coin, but some think streaky sequences are what real chance looks like. So each person has their own signed preference for switching, drawn from a population that can include negative values.\n\n**Why this one:** It goes straight at the critique's strongest finding, the one that survives the FDR correction. About 19% of participants mostly pick the sequence with fewer switches, while the best model predicts about 5%. That model gives everyone the same direction of preference, so it can't represent people who prefer streaky sequences. The brief asked for something simpler or higher-variance than anything in the set, and this is the simplest version: a single switch-rate cue, with each person's weight able to change sign.\n\n**How it's built:**\n- **Feature:** the difference between the two sequences' switch rates, where a switch rate is the share of neighbouring flips that differ.\n- **Per-person weight:** a non-centred hierarchical slope, `mu + sigma·z`, with 400 slots. That leaves spare slots, so the next design can predict new participants.\n- **Prediction:** `p_left = sigmoid(beta[participant] · switch_diff)`, using a standard Bernoulli likelihood.\n\n**Caveats:**\n- **Fit:** the smoke-fit ELPD is far below the best model's −2318, so it will probably lose by a wide margin. The brief accepts that: a loud loss would tell us that a sign difference between people can't stand in for the richer mechanism.\n- **Convergence untested:** the check ran only one chain, so R-hat couldn't be computed. Admission's full fit will test convergence.\n- **PSIS-LOO reliability:** the smoke fit flagged 1.5% of trials with high Pareto k, above the 1.0% tolerance. If that persists in the full fit, the model's ELPD estimate will be marked unreliable.\n- **Novelty:** the check doesn't test this. A one-cue model with per-person signs should predict differently from everything in the set, but admission's novelty gate decides.\n\nFiles are in `/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment2/model_loop/iter_0/candidate_1/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2000,"cache_read_input_tokens":58812,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2000,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"dc44f0df-6886-4691-8206-0862392f4b85","uuid":"c4e1ab80-40cc-4d29-8e2a-124bcf465ce6","timestamp":"2026-10-05T01:54:51.391Z","request_id":"req_011CfiFs56N3GVuoarjuxWHs"}
